125	    <div class="at-a-glance">
126	      <div class="glance-title">At a Glance</div>
127	      <div class="glance-sections">
128	        <div class="glance-section"><strong>What's working:</strong> You treat Claude as a project runner rather than an autocomplete — handing it pre-approved multi-task plans, spec-numbered work with hard exit gates, and letting it dispatch sub-agents through a tracked work queue. That structure paid off: a 45-task Rust monorepo remediation landed with tests green throughout, and the michi-pipeline concurrency work grew to a fully passing suite. You also push back well; demanding an adversarial re-check on the Callisto publishing config surfaced a silent match-pattern bug that would otherwise have shipped quietly. <a href="#section-wins" class="see-more">Impressive Things You Did →</a></div>
129	        <div class="glance-section"><strong>What's hindering you:</strong> On Claude&apos;s side, the recurring pattern is work that looks done but isn&apos;t — configs that never matched, files believed committed that weren&apos;t, and doc links pointing at functions that didn&apos;t exist yet. Gate failures also tended to surface late, costing several fix-and-reconfirm rounds on field-type pinning and surviving mutants. On your side, sub-agent dispatches sometimes went out without the full spec pasted in (they don&apos;t inherit your conversation), and research-heavy sessions tended to end in a plan rather than a diff because application wasn&apos;t scoped as its own pass. <a href="#section-friction" class="see-more">Where Things Go Wrong →</a></div>
130	        <div class="glance-section"><strong>Quick wins to try:</strong> Use Hooks to auto-run your exit gates, `cargo doc` warnings check, and a `git status` cleanliness check after edits — that turns your late-discovered failures into immediate ones. Turn your spec-dispatch template into a Custom Skill so every sub-agent call ships with the full spec text, acceptance criteria, and a standing instruction to adversarially verify config against the real CLI. Start long orchestration sessions with a repo-state snapshot so ground truth is established before the first task lands. <a href="#section-features" class="see-more">Features to Try →</a></div>
131	        <div class="glance-section"><strong>Ambitious workflows:</strong> As models get better at long autonomous runs, push gate execution into the sub-agent&apos;s definition-of-done and add a dedicated gate-fixer per failure class, escalating to you only after repeated failures — a 45-task plan could then land overnight against a single verdict ledger you review in the morning. Make adversarial red-teaming automatic rather than something you remember to request: spawn an agent after every config or build change whose job is to prove the implementation wrong empirically. And consider fanning research out across parallel agents that return structured findings, then fanning back into a spec-editing agent that applies each finding as a concrete diff with provenance — collapsing your research and application sessions into one unattended pass. <a href="#section-horizon" class="see-more">On the Horizon →</a></div>
132	      </div>
