326	    <h2 id="section-usage">How You Use Claude Code</h2>
327	    <div class="narrative">
328	      <p>You operate less like someone typing individual code requests and more like an <strong>engineering manager delegating a work order</strong>. Your sessions are long-running and plan-driven: a 45-task remediation plan for a Rust monorepo, SPEC-005 through SPEC-009 driven to exit gates, a TDD pipeline for parallel execution and cancellation in michi-pipeline. You front-load the specification — the plan already exists and is pre-approved — and then hand Claude the baton and let it run. The tool distribution confirms this: 905 Bash calls, 224 Agent dispatches, 131 TaskUpdates and 70 TaskCreates against only 421 messages total. That ratio means <strong>Claude is doing dozens of autonomous actions per message you send</strong>; you are not micromanaging turn-by-turn, you are supervising an orchestration.</p>
329	<p>But you are far from a passive delegator. Your most distinctive move is the <strong>adversarial audit</strong>. In the Callisto publishing session, the config looked correct and tests were green — and you demanded a re-verification anyway, which surfaced a `callisto.toml` using directory paths for `[[package]]` match values that silently never matched anything. That bug would have shipped. This pattern of not trusting green output shows up repeatedly in your goal mix: research_and_verification and verification_and_audit are your top themes, ahead of raw feature implementation. You also insist on hard, mechanical proof rather than vibes — exit gates, mutant survival counts, 77/77 test tallies, real-CLI validation instead of assumed behavior. When Claude claimed spec JSON files were committed and they weren&apos;t, the discrepancy got caught and reconstituted into a proper two-commit split rather than papered over.</p>
330	<p>You show notable <strong>tolerance for long convergence loops</strong>. SPEC-005 required five-plus retry rounds over field-type pinning; SPEC-006 and 007 each needed fix rounds for surviving mutants. You did not interrupt, redirect, or abandon the approach — you let the gate-fail-fix-reconfirm cycle grind to green. Your friction profile is almost entirely buggy_code and wrong_approach rather than misunderstood-intent, which is the signature of someone whose upfront specs are unambiguous enough that the only failures left are genuine implementation defects. The one place this style costs you: the Lumen research session ended with findings gathered but spec application still in planning, because your scope per session is ambitious enough that a thorough research phase can consume the whole window. Markdown dominates your file activity (399) over Rust (232) — <strong>you spend more effort writing the specs than watching the code</strong>, and that&apos;s precisely why the delegation works.</p>
331	      <div class="key-insight"><strong>Key pattern:</strong> You hand Claude a fully-specified multi-task plan, let it orchestrate autonomously through dozens of tool calls without interruption, then aggressively audit the result and refuse to accept green tests as proof.</div>
332	    </div>
