526	    <h2 id="section-friction">Where Things Go Wrong</h2>
527	    <p class="section-intro">Across long orchestration-heavy sessions you generally get where you&apos;re going, but you repeatedly lose time to silently-wrong configuration, unverified repo state, and sub-agent handoffs that drop context.</p>
528	    <div class="friction-categories">
529	      
530	        <div class="friction-category">
531	          <div class="friction-title">Silent failures caught only by adversarial verification</div>
532	          <div class="friction-desc">Several times, work appeared complete but was quietly wrong — configs that never matched, files that were never committed. You caught these only because you pushed back and demanded re-verification; building that challenge step into your workflow earlier would save the rework.</div>
533	          <ul class="friction-examples"><li>Your callisto.toml used directory paths for [[package]] match values, which silently never matched anything — only surfaced when you demanded an adversarial re-verification of the publishing setup</li><li>Spec JSON files and a plan doc believed to be committed had never actually been committed, forcing mid-session repo-state verification and reconstitution of a two-commit split</li></ul>
534	        </div>
535	      
536	        <div class="friction-category">
537	          <div class="friction-title">Sub-agent context loss and orchestration overhead</div>
538	          <div class="friction-desc">With 224 Agent calls and heavy delegation, you hit the recurring problem that sub-agents don&apos;t inherit your conversation. Pasting full specs and acceptance criteria into every dispatch upfront avoids the repeat calls.</div>
539	          <ul class="friction-examples"><li>A subagent was dispatched without the full spec pasted in, requiring the entire call to be repeated once the missing context was noticed</li><li>Exit-gate FAIL verdicts on SPEC-005 needed 5+ retry rounds over field-type pinning, with SPEC-006/007 each requiring additional fix rounds for surviving mutants</li></ul>
540	        </div>
541	      
542	        <div class="friction-category">
543	          <div class="friction-title">CI gates broken by changes made ahead of their dependencies</div>
544	          <div class="friction-desc">You land code that references things that don&apos;t exist yet, breaking build and doc gates that then need a separate fix cycle. Running the relevant gate locally before landing each task would catch these immediately.</div>
545	          <ul class="friction-examples"><li>A rustdoc intra-doc link added in T02 pointed at a not-yet-existing function, breaking the doc-warnings CI gate until it was separately traced and fixed</li><li>CI/Release failures root-caused to a version-sync mismatch stemming from missing npm publish config — a configuration gap that only surfaced at release time</li></ul>
546	        </div>
